WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.000
In this problem, a building exists on a plot next to which an 8-foot-deep excavation is being made.

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:13.000
The bottom of the excavation is planned to start 15 feet away from the building,

00:00:13.000 --> 00:00:17.000
and will then slope upward until it reaches the ground surface.

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:21.000
According to the OSHA technical manual regarding excavations,

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.000
the soil in this area has been classified as Type C.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:30.000
It is desired to leave some amount of horizontal perimeter strip next to the building.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.000
What is the maximum permissible width of this perimeter strip in feet?

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:44.000
So, all this problem really requires is finding the slope of the slope requirements for Type C soil,

00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:47.000
and then solving for some basic geometry.

00:00:47.000 --> 00:00:52.000
So, we have our excavation depth here, the total distance away from the building in question here,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:58.000
and we need to know how much distance might be available for a strip perimeter strip,

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:07.000
depending on the value of the slope or the steepness of the grid.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:18.000
So, the first thing we can do is we can search either OSHA or Type C into our reference manual,

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:28.000
and that only is to section 3.10.3 slope configurations in layered soils.

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.000
The problem doesn't mention anything about multiple layers of soil,

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:35.000
so we can assume that there is only one layer.

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:41.000
So, as we can see in our table, our excavation for Type C soil is less than 20 feet deep,

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:49.000
and the slope requirements are 1.5 horizontal to one vertical.

00:01:49.000 --> 00:01:55.000
So, we want to know how much room there is for a perimeter strip.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:59.000
So, we need to know the horizontal length of the slope.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:02.000
Solving this equation will give that to us.

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:16.000
So, we know that 1.5 over 1, which is going to be x over y, equals x, which we don't know yet over on this side,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:20.000
and y is going to be 8 feet.

00:02:20.000 --> 00:02:29.000
So, solving for this, x equals 12 feet.

00:02:29.000 --> 00:02:36.000
So, finally, all we have to do, we can find the space available for a perimeter strip

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:44.000
by subtracting the horizontal length of the excavation from the total horizontal distance from the bottom of the excavation to the building.

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:52.000
So, we now know that there is going to be 12 feet of space required,

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:58.000
the horizontal length required for a slope of Type C soil,

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:06.000
meaning that if we have 15 feet and we need 12 feet,

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:13.000
then 3 feet of horizontal distance will be left over here for a perimeter strip.

00:03:13.000 --> 00:03:16.000
And that's all it takes to solve this problem.

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:21.000
Simply knowing where to find your slope for Type C soil, and then how to apply it with some basic math.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.000
And that's it.
